---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gdpCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'c', label: 'consumption C', min: 20, max: 100, step: 1, value: 65 },
    { name: 'i', label: 'investment I', min: 5, max: 40, step: 1, value: 18 },
    { name: 'g', label: 'government G', min: 5, max: 60, step: 1, value: 20 },
    { name: 'nx', label: 'net exports (X − M)', min: -20, max: 20, step: 1, value: -3 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'GDP = C + I + G + NX', expr: 'c + i + g + nx' },
    { label: 'C share of GDP', expr: 'c/(c + i + g + nx)*100', unit: '%' },
    { label: 'trade contribution', expr: 'nx', unit: 'points' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/gdp-national-accounts">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>GDP is the market value of all final goods and services produced in a country in a
    year</strong> — the most-watched number in economics. It is built from four spending streams:
    <strong>C + I + G + NX</strong> (consumption, investment, government, net exports). Knowing
    what each bucket holds — and what GDP deliberately ignores — is the difference between using
    the number and being used by it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="build an economy from its four spending streams — watch shares shift as you drag" widget="sliderLab" config={gdpCfg} />

  <p>
    The readouts show typical rich-country shares: consumption ~60–70%, investment ~15–25%,
    government ~15–25%, trade hovering near zero (exports minus imports). Drag net exports very
    negative and you have a trade-deficit economy; drag government up and you have 1945 Britain.
  </p>

  <h2>What GDP misses — deliberately and accidentally</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deliberately excluded:</strong> second-hand sales, financial trades, intermediate goods (counted once, in the final product), unpaid household work.</li>
    <li><strong>Not subtracted:</strong> pollution, resource depletion, disaster cleanup — a cleanup <em>adds</em> to GDP. Broken-window fallacy in national accounting form.</li>
    <li><strong>Distribution is invisible:</strong> GDP per capita is an average; averages float above the median. A country can grow while most citizens stagnate.</li>
    <li><strong>Alternatives exist:</strong> GDP per capita PPP (purchasing-power adjusted — the fair international comparison), HDI (health + education), and wellbeing indices. Kuznets, GDP's 1934 inventor, warned Congress it was never a welfare measure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kuznets report to US Congress (1934); OpenStax, Principles of Economics; OECD Better Life Initiative.</p>
</TopicPage>
